<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taras Shevchenko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a>–<a href="/years/1861">1861</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Taras Hryhorovych Shevchenko</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a> Shevchenko was a Ukrainian poet, writer, artist and ethnographer whose poetry collection Kobzar (1840) is regarded as the foundation of modern Ukrainian literature and, to some degree, of the modern Ukrainian language. Born a serf in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> under Russian rule, he was bought free in 1838 with 2,500 rubles raised by a lottery for Karl Briullov's portrait of Vasily Zhukovsky, and studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-academy">Imperial Academy</a> of Arts. Arrested in 1847 with the Brotherhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Saints Cyril and Methodius</a>, he was exiled as a private to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orenburg">Orenburg</a> and Orsk, forbidden to write or paint. Amnestied in 1857, he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> and was reburied at Kaniv.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taras-shevchenko.jpg" alt="Taras Shevchenko" width="330" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrey Denyer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Т._Г._Шевченко._Квітень_1859.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Taras Shevchenko was born on 9 March 1814 (25 February in the old style) in Morintsy, a village in Kiev Governorate, about 20 years after the third partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> brought the territory under Russian rule. His parents were serf peasants who worked land owned by Vasily Engelhardt. When Engelhardt died in 1828, his son Pavel became the family's landlord and trained the 14-year-old Taras as a servant. In 1829 Taras travelled in Engelhardt's retinue to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and Vilno. When Engelhardt caught him painting a portrait of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matvei-platov">Matvei Platov</a> at night, he had the boy whipped, but later apprenticed him to a painter-decorator, who recommended lessons from the Polish painter Franciszek Ksawery Lampi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> the young Ukrainian artist Ivan Soshenko gave Shevchenko lessons and introduced him around June 1832 to the painter Karl Briullov. Briullov asked Engelhardt for Shevchenko's freedom and was refused. Shevchenko later became a student of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-academy">Imperial Academy</a> of Arts with Briullov as his mentor. He travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 1843, 1845 and 1846, and in October 1843 wrote the poem The Dug Grave. In 1845 he became a drawing teacher at Kiev University and met members of the Brotherhood of Saints Cyril and Methodius.</p><p>He was arrested on 5 April 1847 and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> for his association with the Brotherhood; he continued to create art and poetry despite prohibitions. He received an amnesty from the new emperor in 1857 but was not permitted to settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> until 1858. In June 1859 he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> for the last time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shevchenko composed his last poem, left unfinished, on 14 February 1861 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> on 10 March 1861. His friends carried out the wish he expressed in his poem Testament: his remains were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and on 22 May 1861 he was reburied on Monk's Hill, now Taras Hill, in Kaniv. Of 237 poems he wrote, only 28 were published in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> during his life. He wrote his poetry in Ukrainian and his prose in Russian. His poetry, above all the Kobzar, is regarded as the foundation of modern Ukrainian literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taras Shevchenko” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Taras Shevchenko</b>, born a serf in 1814, was made a house servant of his lord at fourteen and whipped for drawing at night; his talent won him friends in Saint Petersburg, who raised 2,500 roubles in 1838 to buy his freedom.</blockquote><a href="/societies/russian-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Russian Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The poem was first published in 1863 in the fourth issue of the Lviv journal Meta, which wrongly attributed it to <b>Taras Shevchenko</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/national-anthem-of-ukraine">State Anthem of Ukraine</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Moryntsi, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.26,31.01&amp;year=1844" aria-label="Moryntsi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5265 446 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Taras Shevchenko"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5715 716h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5715 716h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.26,31.01&amp;year=1844">What was here in 1844? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
